        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          Objectives<div>Long-duration orbital space-flights are associated with increased levels of psychological stress, acute and 
chronic exposure to space radiation and microgravity-induced changes, all of which are known to detrimentally 
impact the immune system. To do so, biologic specimen (blood) will be obtained from the subject. The 
epigenomic changes induced by prolonged exposure to microgravity and cosmic irradiation will be compared to 
alterations occurring under normal gravity in control subjects. This approach will identify pathways involved in 
altered immune response during long-duration orbital space-flights and establish specific hypotheses 
concerning potential targets for therapeutic intervention</div><div><br></div><div>It is proposed to investigate global changes in the enhancers responsible for coordinating intrinsic and extrinsic 
differentiation signals of immune cells after a long-term orbital space-flight. To achieve this, the following 
objectives shall be considered: 
1. Epigenomic profiling of selected immune cell populations pre- and post-flight. Here a mapping of the 
enhancer landscape pre- and post-flight in specific cell types of the immune system will be done.
2. Transcriptomic profiling of selected immune cell populations pre- and post-flight. Here a correlation of the 
alterations in the enhancer landscape to changes in the transcriptome will be performed.
3. Bioinformatic identification of enhancer signatures functionally relevant to orbital space-flights and 
integration of these signatures with known pathologies. Here a comparative analysis of enhancer 
signatures identified across different subjects will be done, which differs in the control subjects comparing 
crew’s data to a control group.
4. Establishment of potential avenues for diagnosis and therapeutic intervention of immune dysregulation 
during long orbital space flights. Here a meta-analysis of space-flight associated enhancer signatures to a 
large cohort of publicly available datasets will be performed, aiming to identify commonalities of the space 
flight associated signatures to other known diseases.</div><div><br></div><div>Approach</div><div>Session windows shall be distributed as follows:
- One pre-flight session between 180 and 60 days before launch.
- One early post-flight session at R+0/+1 days.
- One post-flight session at R+45 (+/-15 days). 
Blood donation of ~24mL for all sessions are required. 
Exceptionally, it is accepted to collect only ~16mL during first post-flight BDC if the science complement for the 
first week after return is too heavy for the crewmember in terms of blood donation requests.
Early Post-flight requirements (within the first week after return) justification: 
A blood draw is required immediately after return. The epigenetic modifications in general, especially those 
associated with enhancer regions, are reversible and highly dynamic. These modifications regulate gene 
expression and have to respond quickly to changes in environmental signals. Therefore, they could in theory 
rapidly revert to normal after return to earth. To avoid a reduction of the observed differential effects, it is 
requested to collect blood samples as early as possible after landing. 
A late session ~one month after return will then be implemented to investigate dynamics after return.</div>
        </div>

